  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nUnseasonably Warm and Dry Through Saturday...\n\nThe upper-level pattern begins the period with an amplified, \nslow-moving ridge centered along the East Coast through \nSaturday, with some wobbling of the ridge axis as a couple of \nstrong shortwaves top the ridge over the Great Lakes and Canada.\nAt the surface, a strong 1030+ mb high will remain positioned \nalong the eastern seaboard, maintaining steady southwest flow \nacross central Illinois. This setup will support unseasonably \nwarm temperatures through Saturday. High temperatures will \ngenerally top out around 70 degrees under sunny skies, while \novernight lows remain mild in the 40s to lower 50s through \nSaturday night.\n\nTurning Colder Sunday Into Next Week...\n\nBy Saturday, a pair of strong shortwaves are progged to dig \nacross the Canadian Prairies and Desert Southwest, respectively,\neventually phasing as they track east across the mid and upper \nMississippi Valley on Sunday. An elongated surface low/trough \nwill develop in response and cross the local area late Saturday \nnight into Sunday, delivering the next opportunity for \nwidespread precipitation. Guidance suggests weak instability may\naccompany the frontal passage (GFS advertises pockets of 50-75 \nJ/kg MUCAPE), but forecast soundings indicate poor mid-level \nlapse rates will keep instability shallow and below the ice \ngrowth zone, making thunderstorms unlikely. QPF looks to remain \nlight with the frontal passage, offering little relief to \nongoing regional drought conditions. NBM 24-hour mean QPF \nsuggests widespread totals around 0.25 inches, though 90th \npercentile guidance hints at isolated swaths of 0.75 to 1.00 \ninch where sustained convective updrafts can tap into the meager\ninstability.\n\nAnother 1035 mb high will build behind the departing system \nover the northern Great Plains Sunday night, spreading into the \nUpper Midwest Monday into Monday night. A tight northerly \npressure gradient ahead of the high will drive colder air into \ncentral Illinois, sending lows down to around the freezing mark \nSunday night with daytime highs only recovering into the 40s on \nMonday. Temperatures will cool further through early next week \nunder prevailing dry conditions beneath the surface ridge.\n\nBy mid-week, deterministic GFS and ECMWF runs depict a strong \nshortwave digging across the lower Mississippi Valley. \nSignificant model discrepancies remain regarding evolution: the \nECMWF carves out a deeper closed low that meanders and lifts \nnorthward, bringing precip chances into central Illinois, \nwhereas the progressive GFS keeps precipitation well to our \nsouth. Given the event is roughly a week out, details will \ncontinue to be refined in upcoming forecast packages.",
